        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: add portable Kubernetes relay",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n- Extract the relay API and security behavior into a shared `@openclaw/reef-relay-core` package.\n- Keep the Cloudflare Worker adapter on that shared contract.\n- Add a Node/PostgreSQL relay, container build, and Helm chart for Kubernetes and OpenShift.\n- Add deployment and self-hosting documentation.\n\n## Why\n\nReef deployments that cannot use Cloudflare need a portable relay while preserving the existing Reef protocol, end-to-end encryption model, and `/v1/` behavior.\n\n## Compatibility and operational safety\n\n- Existing Cloudflare deployments remain supported through the refactored Worker adapter.\n- Both adapters run the shared relay contract suite.\n- Helm requires an explicit non-`latest` image tag so upgrades change the pod template and the migration and serving containers use the same version.\n- The trusted-proxy list is empty by default. Forwarded client IPs are accepted only when the immediate TCP peer matches an explicitly configured address or CIDR, with focused spoofing coverage.\n- PostgreSQL migrations use an advisory lock and a migration ledger so concurrent relay startup is serialized and idempotent.\n- The release-owned changelog is left untouched.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `pnpm -r build`\n- `pnpm -r test`\n- `helm lint deploy/helm/reef-relay --set image.tag=test-v1`\n- `helm template reef deploy/helm/reef-relay --set image.tag=test-v1`\n- Verified that Helm rejects a missing image tag and `latest`.\n- Ran PostgreSQL 14 locally with the standalone migration entrypoint and two relay processes: all nine Node relay tests passed, including trusted-proxy spoofing cases, encrypted delivery and acknowledgement, replay rejection, WebSocket push, and cross-replica socket replacement.\n- CI provisions PostgreSQL, runs the standalone migration entrypoint, and starts two relay processes, so the PostgreSQL and cross-replica suites no longer pass by being skipped.\n\n## Remaining review decision\n\nThis adds a second relay platform and persistent backend to Reef core. A maintainer should explicitly confirm that Reef will own the Kubernetes/PostgreSQL adapter and its compatibility contract.\n\nBefore merge, please also attach redacted evidence from the existing cluster test showing a Helm install or upgrade, migration completion, readiness, restart behavior, and end-to-end cross-pod delivery.",
        "branch": "feat/kubernetes-relay",
